{
  "id": 529816098,
  "iid": 26436,
  "project_id": 7898047,
  "title": "ICMPv6: Dissect ICMP extensions in Extended Echo Reply",
  "description": "Dissect the ICMP extension structure carried in an ICMPv6 Extended Echo Reply (type 161), the same way the Extended Echo Request (type 160) already does.\n\n## Why\n\nPer RFC 8335, the Extended Echo Reply is an extensible message: a reply longer than its fixed 8-byte header carries an ICMP extension structure (e.g. an Interface Identification Object echoed back by the responder). The request path already dissects these extensions, but the reply path stopped after the State/Active/IPv4/IPv6 bits, leaving the trailing extension bytes undissected.\n\nThis also brings ICMPv6 to parity with ICMPv4, where both the Extended Echo Request and Reply (type 42/43) already dissect extensions (added in !19325 / commit 709e6905).\n\n## Change\n\nIn `dissect_icmpv6()`, the `ICMP6_EXTECHOREPLY` case now hands any bytes past the fixed header to the shared `icmp_extension` dissector — mirroring the `ICMP6_EXTECHO` (request) case. Guarded so replies with no extension are unaffected.\n\n## Before / after (tshark -V on a type-161 reply)\n\nBefore: decode stops at the IPv6 bit; extension bytes ignored.\n\nAfter: ICMP Multi-Part Extensions Interface Identification Object Class: Interface Identification Object (3) C-Type: 1 (Identifies Interface By Name) Name: et-0/0/1.0\n\n## References\n\n- RFC 8335 (PROBE: Extended Echo Request/Reply)\n- RFC 4884 (ICMP Multi-Part Messages / extension structure)\n- ICMPv4 precedent: !19325, commit 709e6905",
