      "id": "3c02a74d1a8c3f64ff5b1f41da4af6a10aee99f4",
      "short_id": "3c02a74d",
      "created_at": "2026-09-13T20:16:47.000+02:00",
      "parent_ids": [
        "c54f437afa248bf56730ff6e4b4ec97869dba011"
      ],
      "title": "ICMPv6: Dissect ICMP extensions in Extended Echo Reply",
      "message": "ICMPv6: Dissect ICMP extensions in Extended Echo Reply\n\nPer RFC 8335, the ICMP Extended Echo Reply (type 161) is an extensible\nmessage: a reply longer than its fixed 8-byte header carries an ICMP\nextension structure, just like the Extended Echo Request (type 160).\nThe request path already dissects these extensions, but the reply path\ndid not, so an Interface Identification Object echoed back by a\nresponder (e.g. the probed interface name/index/address) was left\nundissected.\n\nDissect the trailing extension structure in the Extended Echo Reply,\nmirroring the Extended Echo Request. This also brings ICMPv6 in line\nwith the existing ICMPv4 Extended Echo Request/Reply (type 42/43)\nbehavior, which already dissects extensions on both.\n",
      "author_name": "surakshah",
      "author_email": "surakshah@juniper.net",
      "authored_date": "2026-09-01T13:03:01.000+05:30",
      "committer_name": "AndersBroman",
